<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abd al-Nabi ibn Ali ibn Mahdi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1176</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abd al-Nabi ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-mahdi">Ali ibn Mahdi</a> was the second ruler of the Mahdid dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, succeeding his father, who had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> from the Najahids in 1159. He ran a militant state that, according to hostile sources, treated other Muslims as unbelievers, and he campaigned widely in Yemen. The Ayyubid prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turanshah">Turanshah</a> conquered Zabid in 1174 and later had him executed.</p></div>
